  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-6 tracking-tight">
      How Many Boxes of Vinyl Plank Flooring Do I Need? (Formula &amp; Sizing Chart)
    </h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5 leading-relaxed">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Calculating how many boxes of Luxury Vinyl Plank (LVP) or SPC rigid core flooring to buy involves three key steps: determining net room square footage, applying a pattern-specific waste percentage, and dividing by the square footage packaged in each individual manufacturer carton.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Calculate Your Exact LVP Boxes &amp; Planks Instantly</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4 text-sm">Enter your room length, width, and box coverage to get exact box counts, 1/4" perimeter spacers, transition T-moldings, and cost estimates.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/vinyl-plank-flooring" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-2.5 px-5 rounded-lg text-sm transition-colors shadow-xs">
          Open Vinyl Plank Flooring Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Standard LVP Box Calculation Formula</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike sheet vinyl, vinyl planks are sold in rigid cardboard cartons typically containing between 18.0 and 24.0 square feet per box. Always round up to the next full whole box:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm my-4 text-center">
        Total Boxes to Buy = Ceiling [ (Room Length × Room Width × Waste Factor) ÷ Sq Ft per Box ]
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">LVP Box Estimating Chart (Assuming 20.0 Sq Ft / Box)</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="min-w-full text-xs text-left border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
          <thead class="bg-gray-100 font-bold text-gray-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Room Dimensions</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Net Area</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Area (+10% Waste)</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Boxes Needed</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Total Purchased Area</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700">
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">10 ft × 10 ft</td><td class="p-3">100 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">110 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">6 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">120 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">12 ft × 12 ft</td><td class="p-3">144 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">158 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">8 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">160 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">12 ft × 16 ft</td><td class="p-3">192 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">211 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">11 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">220 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">14 ft × 18 ft</td><td class="p-3">252 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">277 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">14 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">280 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">16 ft × 20 ft</td><td class="p-3">320 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">352 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">18 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">360 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">20 ft × 25 ft</td><td class="p-3">500 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">550 sq ft</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">28 Boxes</td><td class="p-3">560 sq ft</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Recommended Waste Percentages by Layout Pattern</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Straight Running Stagger (1/3 Offset):</strong> <strong>7% to 10% waste</strong>. Standard DIY practice where cut-off ends starting one row become the starter piece on the next row.</li>
        <li><strong>Rooms with Multiple Doors / Closets:</strong> <strong>10% to 12% waste</strong> to account for complex door jamb undercuts.</li>
        <li><strong>45-Degree Diagonal Layout:</strong> <strong>12% to 15% waste</strong> due to triangle offcuts along all perimeter walls.</li>
        <li><strong>Herringbone or Chevron Pattern:</strong> <strong>15% to 18% waste</strong>. Requires specific left/right locking planks with extensive 45° corner trimming.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Why Keeping 1 Extra Unopened Box Is Essential</h2>
      <p>
        Flooring dye lots change frequently between manufacturing runs. Even if you purchase the exact same brand and color SKU two years later, new batches often exhibit slight sheen variations or minor tongue-and-groove millwork differences. Keeping 1 full spare box in a closet ensures you have matching replacement planks if a future water heater leak or heavy dropped object damages a plank.
      </p>

